WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Let u be odd and greater than one.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.073
Each coefficient of the four sign
polynomials is positive or negative one.

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:11.240
These same polynomials define c, d, and g.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.800
Node five formed exact
polynomial quotients a and b:

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.960
d minus c equals two a, while g minus c
equals the product of one plus i and b.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.020
The coefficients of a are integers,
and those of b are Gaussian integers.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.780
These divisions were
made before reduction.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.760
Keep one evaluation ring throughout.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.180
Let B be the integers with i and
the chosen prime roots adjoined,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.600
and let A be B with one over u adjoined.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.200
For a subset S of the primes dividing u,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.600
multiply its chosen roots
to obtain zeta sub S.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.460
Write c sub S for c at that
input, and similarly for d and g.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.180
The preceding node proves
that all three are units in A,

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:01.100
and that A modulo one plus
i is nonzero. In particular,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.420
c sub S has inverse its complex conjugate
divided by u squared, belonging to A.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.620
Evaluate the two exact identities and
keep this unit denominator visible.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.300
Define r sub S as d sub
S divided by c sub S,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.860
and w sub S as g sub S divided by c

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.220
sub S. Subtract one from the first
ratio. Combine the fractions,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.480
then replace d sub S minus c sub
S by two times a at zeta sub S.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.700
The result is two times a at
that input divided by c sub S.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.260
Repeat with the second ratio: w sub S
minus one equals the factor one plus i,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.160
multiplied by b at that
input and divided by c sub S.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.420
Both remaining quotients belong to
A, because the denominator is a unit.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.360
Two equals negative i times
the square of one plus i,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.940
so both displayed factors
vanish modulo one plus i.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.320
The reduced differences are zero.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.620
Therefore r sub S and w sub
S each have residue one.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.180
Every division was by a unit; we
did not divide by a zero residue.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.380
Now unpack the Delta
comparisons into alternating

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.540
products of the r ratios and the w ratios,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:24.000
with positive exponents for even subsets
and negative exponents for odd subsets.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.880
Every factor is a unit of residue one.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.680
Its inverse has residue one too: reduce
the product of a unit and its inverse,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.540
and their product is still one.
Since the first residue is one,

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.500
the inverse residue must also
be one. Thus every factor in

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.480
both alternating products reduces to
one, including the inverse factors.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:49.400
Both capital R and capital W have
residue one modulo one plus i.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.920
For u equal to three, the two inputs are
one and omega, a primitive cube root.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.030
Capital R is r at the empty subset divided
by r at the subset containing three;

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.940
capital W has the same form using w.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.000
More distinct primes give more factors.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.640
The same product and inverse
rule applies to all of them.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.560
This is an algebraic residue condition.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.340
The exact comparisons still
await odd-order rigidity.
